Interpretation
- All 160 tagged GetWorldSpace completions were matched to their actual joined BSJobs list. Of 83 calls overlapping the final DynDOLOD stack's lock wait, 76 had published after an earlier native drain cutoff. The matching joined wait was on the prior drain thread and render frame in all 76. Of those, 72 still had an earlier FIFO message. The observed head stack at join was the first stack popped in the next stock drain in all 76 cases.
- Four of the 76 were the exact stock GetWorldSpace query at FIFO head and retained the original DynDOLOD root lock. None owned the VM freeze guard or were in the stock native phase. All 1506 stock drain starts owned that guard. No observed joined wait owned it.
- Those four exact heads waited 15.770–16.739 ms from join to stock native dispatch. No Papyrus batch was submitted between the observed join and next native drain. The root lock was released 0.011–0.017 ms after the next tasklet batch was submitted in all four. This is an observed stock sequence, not proof of how a new phase would behave. Dispatch alone after the already-joined batch would still leave the continuation for a later tasklet pass unless scheduling changed again.
- No tested boundary combines full join, the exact FIFO head and the stock native execution context. Taking a new guard and adding a new native pass would be a new synchronization arrangement. No acceleration was installed and the performance figures are stock baselines. All four windows completed and PresentMon exited normally.

